Cleaning Technologies Continue to Evolve

The market includes megasonic cleaning, ultrasonic cleaning, jet-spray cleaning, brush scrubbing and other techniques. Each approach addresses different contamination types, wafer geometries and process requirements.

Megasonic cleaning can help remove particles from delicate surfaces, while brush scrubbing and spray-based approaches serve other cleaning requirements. The appropriate method depends on the material being processed, feature dimensions, chemical compatibility and the possibility of structural damage.

Equipment manufacturers must optimize cleaning performance alongside throughput, water consumption, chemical usage and waste management.

Qualification Requirements and Capital Costs Remain Challenges

Wet cleaning equipment must be qualified against specific manufacturing processes. Changing equipment or recipes can require extensive testing to confirm that cleaning performance, defect levels and production yields remain acceptable.

This qualification burden can slow supplier adoption and make it difficult for new entrants to displace established vendors. The market is also exposed to semiconductor capital expenditure cycles, which can influence the timing of new equipment purchases.

Price competition and the need to maintain consistent performance place additional pressure on equipment suppliers.

